      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"choose-the-model\">Choose the model</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Every function of EBaMl that calls an LLM takes a <code>model</code> argument. This notebook sends the same request to\nseveral providers, so that you can compare the setup of each one and the text that each one writes.</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Everything outside the buttons runs without a key and sends no request.</li>\n<li>A button sends real requests (three turns for one graph) to the provider of its section. Press it only for a\n  provider that you have set up. A section shows the key that it needs, and whether the environment has it.</li>\n<li>The key never goes into <code>ModelOptions</code>. The options hold the <em>name</em> of the environment variable, and EBaMl reads\n  the variable when it sends a request. To set a key, start the notebook from a shell that has the variable.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The documentation page \"Choosing the model\" lists all providers and options.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "vblA",
      "code_hash": "77976530320f7a8b4c3d3ded072fd7e5",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"a-small-ebm-to-talk-about\">A small EBM to talk about</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The Titanic data and one feature, <code>sex</code>, whose graph has two bins. A short graph keeps each request cheap.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
